{{--
    The hero ribbon.

    Stripe's is a length of silk, not a cloud: what makes it read that way is
    that it is built from hundreds of thin strands lying side by side, so the
    eye picks up the striations along the flow. The old version was four
    blurred gradient ellipses, which is why it read as fog no matter how the
    colours were tuned -- there was no structure in it to see.

    So: three fans of stroked bezier curves. Colour runs ACROSS each fan
    (cool on the inner edge, warm on the outer), never along a single strand,
    which is what makes a bundle of separate lines read as one twisted sheet.
    The body and the fold cross, and the overlap is where the colour goes
    deep, the way two layers of silk darken where they meet.

    Everything here is static geometry: no filter: blur() anywhere, so the
    marketing heavy-animated-blur guard stays green and nothing is
    re-composited on scroll. The file is generated, not hand-written -- the
    paths come from a fan function, which is why the numbers march.

    preserveAspectRatio="none" on purpose: the viewBox is mapped 1:1 onto the
    hero box so the composition is designed in the coordinates it lands in.
    Strands stretch with the box, which for organic curves reads as the
    ribbon foreshortening rather than as distortion.
--}}
